WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:07.233
Every day, people get up, go to school or work, walk, read, and do many other things.

2
00:00:07.234 --> 00:00:09.203
Where do we get the energy?

3
00:00:09.204 --> 00:00:16.756
The answer lies in what we eat, drink, and breathe; our fuel is food, water, and air.

4
00:00:16.757 --> 00:00:19.499
But what about non-living things?

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.044
What powers a car? What makes water boil?

6
00:00:23.045 --> 00:00:26.108
Where does your computer get its energy from?

7
00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:32.350
"I put gasoline in the car, cook over a gas stove, and plug in the computer," you reply.

8
00:00:32.351 --> 00:00:39.688
Your answer sums up the main energy sources that power non-living things: oil, gas, and electricity.

9
00:00:39.689 --> 00:00:50.508
Oil and gas have two energy uses; the first is to fuel cars, buses, trains, and planes, and the second is to generate electricity.

10
00:00:50.509 --> 00:00:58.254
Coal, nuclear power, and hydropower (energy from flowing water) are also used to make electricity.

11
00:00:58.255 --> 00:01:03.315
People in most developed countries use oil for 50% of their energy.

12
00:01:03.316 --> 00:01:09.576
Problems arise, however, when we rely so heavily on oil for our energy needs.

13
00:01:09.577 --> 00:01:13.084
First of all, there is a limited supply of oil.

14
00:01:13.085 --> 00:01:19.730
Oil industry experts estimate that current oil reserves will last for only forty more years.

15
00:01:19.731 --> 00:01:27.070
This means that by 2045, there will be no more oil to make gasoline or electricity.

16
00:01:27.071 --> 00:01:33.045
Currently, most of the world's available oil is consumed only by developed countries.

17
00:01:33.046 --> 00:01:43.732
Nearly a third of the world's population of 6.1 billion people has no electricity, and another third has only limited access to electricity.

18
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:49.874
Twenty percent of the world's population consumes seventy percent of the world's energy resources.

19
00:01:49.875 --> 00:01:54.704
What will happen when developing countries get access to electricity and oil?

20
00:01:54.705 --> 00:01:57.204
There simply won't be enough.

21
00:01:57.205 --> 00:02:00.610
Another problem is the location of our energy.

22
00:02:00.611 --> 00:02:08.611
As recently as a couple of centuries ago, virtually everyone used fuel that they could find close to home.

23
00:02:08.612 --> 00:02:14.595
In the modern world, energy sources are often long distances away from the end user.

24
00:02:14.596 --> 00:02:20.337
For example, many countries have no oil reserves and import all of their oil.

25
00:02:20.338 --> 00:02:29.846
When oil is transported over long distances, there is the risk of oil spills, which can cause irreversible damage to the natural environment.

26
00:02:29.847 --> 00:02:36.655
Conflicts, or even wars, can erupt over the control of oil fields and oil pipelines.

27
00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:41.893
The most serious problem with burning oil for energy is global warming.

28
00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:49.150
The earth’s atmosphere is getting hotter because there is too much carbon dioxide (CO2) in the air.

29
00:02:49.151 --> 00:02:59.153
Burning fuels like gasoline (to drive cars) and coal (to produce electricity) releases large amounts of CO2 into the air.

30
00:02:59.154 --> 00:03:11.212
Scientists predict that global warming may cause sea levels to rise, animals to become extinct, and extreme weather, everything from severe droughts to hurricanes.

31
00:03:11.213 --> 00:03:17.386
The good news is that we don't have to use oil, gas, and coal to produce energy.

32
00:03:17.387 --> 00:03:26.140
There are other energy sources that won't run out, don't require transportation across the globe, and don't release CO2.

33
00:03:26.141 --> 00:03:37.629
The best thing about energy from these alternative energy sources, also called "renewable energy," is that it is unlimited─it comes from water, wind, and the sun.

34
00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:44.701
Wind power is not new; boats and ships have been using it for thousands of years to travel across the globe.

35
00:03:44.702 --> 00:03:51.542
Windmills in Europe have used wind for centuries to pump water, grind grain, and saw wood.

36
00:03:51.543 --> 00:04:01.345
In modern times, machines called wind turbines, with blades from one meter to one hundred meters long, turn wind into electricity.

37
00:04:01.346 --> 00:04:12.516
Scientists believe that if collections of these turbines were installed in wind farms in the world’s windiest places, there would be enough energy to satisfy global demand.

38
00:04:12.517 --> 00:04:15.675
Solar power is energy from the sun.

39
00:04:15.676 --> 00:04:22.241
Energy from the sun can be used in two ways: to make electricity and to heat water.

40
00:04:22.242 --> 00:04:28.167
Solar cells are thin pieces of silicon that produce electricity when exposed to sunlight.

41
00:04:28.168 --> 00:04:31.822
You may have used a solar-powered calculator or flashlight.

42
00:04:31.823 --> 00:04:35.402
Solar cells can also power lights in a home.

43
00:04:35.403 --> 00:04:39.086
A solar-powered car has even been built!

44
00:04:39.087 --> 00:04:45.324
In Australia, a solar boat was showcased at the 2000 Olympic Games in Sydney.

45
00:04:45.325 --> 00:04:53.884
The Solar Sailor is powered by solar panels, and its movable, wing-like solar panels also function as sails.

46
00:04:53.885 --> 00:04:58.564
The sun's energy can also heat water efficiently and cheaply.

47
00:04:58.565 --> 00:05:10.541
Water is passed through solar collectors that concentrate the sun's rays and heat the water; the hot water is then stored in a tank and can be used for bathing or washing dishes.

48
00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:15.033
Other kinds of renewable energy are also under development.

49
00:05:15.034 --> 00:05:19.623
One of these comes from biomass, which means organic matter.

50
00:05:19.624 --> 00:05:34.691
Wood has been used for thousands of years to create energy, but scientists have discovered that other kinds of organic matter can also be used to create fuel, like certain grasses, trees, plants, or even animal dung.

51
00:05:34.692 --> 00:05:41.677
Biomass can be treated and made into fuel, or it can produce electricity as it decomposes.

52
00:05:41.678 --> 00:05:48.423
Another renewable energy source is geothermal energy, energy that comes from inside the earth.

53
00:05:48.424 --> 00:05:55.692
For example, steam from geysers and water from hot springs can be used to generate electricity.

54
00:05:55.693 --> 00:06:02.685
At geothermal power plants in California, geysers produce steam, which turns electric turbines.

55
00:06:02.686 --> 00:06:07.323
Some of these power generators have been operating for over forty years.

56
00:06:07.324 --> 00:06:14.490
Hydrogen is a source of clean energy that is very attractive because it is the most abundant element in the universe.

57
00:06:14.491 --> 00:06:22.908
A fuel cell combines hydrogen and oxygen to produce electricity and heat─and the only by-product is pure water!

58
00:06:22.909 --> 00:06:29.892
Hydrogen fuel cells have the potential to replace the engines in vehicles and provide power for cars and buses.

59
00:06:29.893 --> 00:06:37.297
Many portable devices could also be powered by these fuel cells, such as laptop computers and cell phones.

60
00:06:37.298 --> 00:06:42.233
A final type of alternative energy source under development is compressed air.

61
00:06:42.234 --> 00:06:47.797
Compressed air is the energy source for the "air car," which uses no gasoline.

62
00:06:47.798 --> 00:06:52.116
Instead, a tank full of compressed air powers the engine.

63
00:06:52.117 --> 00:06:55.862
The tank needs to be refilled at an "air" station.

64
00:06:55.863 --> 00:07:02.212
An air car factory has already been established in France, and production will begin soon.

65
00:07:02.213 --> 00:07:14.781
So, if energy from oil, coal, and gas causes problems like pollution and global warming, to name only a few, why don't we switch to renewable energy immediately?

66
00:07:14.782 --> 00:07:18.852
Why doesn't every house have solar-heated hot water?

67
00:07:18.853 --> 00:07:22.672
Why aren't our computers powered by wind turbines?

68
00:07:22.673 --> 00:07:26.108
Why doesn’t everyone drive an air car?

69
00:07:26.109 --> 00:07:28.751
There are a number of reasons.

70
00:07:28.752 --> 00:07:34.598
First, renewable energy is currently more expensive than traditional fuels.

71
00:07:34.599 --> 00:07:41.844
Second, decades of access to cheap oil have made consumers lazy and apathetic about energy.

72
00:07:41.845 --> 00:07:52.157
"There's enough oil now, who cares about the future?" and "As long as my city isn't polluted, it's okay," are common attitudes.

73
00:07:52.158 --> 00:08:00.149
Third, at present, the disadvantages of oil are still not grave enough to force citizens or governments to react.

74
00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:11.907
Pollution is an ever-increasing problem, but its effects are gradual, and the most severe effects of global warming may not be seen for another fifty years.

75
00:08:11.908 --> 00:08:22.393
Finally, companies and governments in the world's most industrialized nations currently earn large profits from the oil, coal, and gas industries.

76
00:08:22.394 --> 00:08:28.704
It is not in their interest to switch to renewable energy sources unless the public demands it.

77
00:08:28.705 --> 00:08:34.291
The good news, however, is that many people around the world are changing their habits.

78
00:08:34.292 --> 00:08:43.787
For example, some schools, community groups, and small businesses are using wind turbines and solar panels as their sources of electricity.

79
00:08:43.788 --> 00:08:51.532
Governments are slowly changing regulations to make it easier for builders to construct homes that rely on renewable energy.

80
00:08:51.533 --> 00:08:57.693
The earth has taken good care of us─it gives us clean air, water, and food to eat.

81
00:08:57.694 --> 00:09:03.274
By switching to renewable sources of energy, we can start to take care of the earth.

